WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.070
Good morning Hank.

00:00:01.070 --> 00:00:02.070
It's Tuesday.

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:04.610
So, you recently made a video outside, and
I was like, "We have outside in Indiana."

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:05.610
So, yeah, here I am.

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:07.800
One of my favorite words in English is "mortification".

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:12.719
It's also one of my least favorite things
to experience, and last week, I was well and

00:00:12.719 --> 00:00:19.250
truly mortified when I used the word "hectacre",
like, 135 times in my video about agriculture

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:20.250
in Ethiopia.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:26.570
I said "hectacre", like, one who takes hec
when in fact, I meant hectare, meaning one

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:31.560
hundred ares, with are being a unit of measure
that means one hundred square meters.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.440
Now, admittedly, units measuring land area:
not among my fields of expertise, but still,

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:39.030
I was trying to share with people what I'd
learned about how to make more food available

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:42.370
to hungry people, but obviously, I knew absolutely
nothing because hectacre!

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:47.000
Hank, I've only experienced real mortification
a couple times in my adult life, like my first

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.620
book signing after Looking for Alaska was
published was only attended by two people.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:55.300
Now, that was obviously concerning, but it
wasn't really mortifying until I was halfway

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:59.640
through my reading, and I read a sentence
from the book that went, "The only thing worse

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:05.239
than having a party that no one attends is
having a party attended by two vastly, deeply

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:09.320
uninteresting people," thereby managing to
insult the only two people nice enough to

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:10.320
come to my signing.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.810
Then, a few years later, I was giving a talk
to several hundred high school students, none

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:15.450
of whom had any idea who I was, and I talked
for forty-five minutes.

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:16.450
Everyone was laughing a lot.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:18.939
They seemed to be having a great time, and
then, finally, I was like, "Are there any

00:01:18.939 --> 00:01:19.939
questions?"

00:01:19.939 --> 00:01:23.280
at which point several dozen people raised
their hands, which is very unusual, and I

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.280
was like, "Yeah, yes, do you have a question?"

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:28.140
and they were like, "Yeah, Mr. Green, are
you aware that your fly is open?"

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.329
Now, Hank, to be clear, it wasn't that my
zipper was down.

00:01:30.329 --> 00:01:31.649
My fly was open.

00:01:31.649 --> 00:01:33.140
Like, you could see my underwear.

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:36.409
And then, I was like, "No, I wasn't aware
that my fly was down."

00:01:36.409 --> 00:01:40.229
I turned around, I zipped up my fly, I turned
back around, and I was like, "Are there any

00:01:40.229 --> 00:01:41.229
other questions?"

00:01:41.229 --> 00:01:42.229
Silence.

00:01:42.229 --> 00:01:44.420
Wait, have I told that story in a Vlogbrothers
video before?

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:46.710
Now, I feel a little bit of mortification
creeping in again.

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:47.710
Blergh.

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:48.710
I don't even know what's been in my own videos.

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:49.930
But anyway, back to hectacre.

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:53.060
So, Sarah was trying to comfort me and she
was like, "You know, nerdfighters don't care

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:54.060
that much about this stuff.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:55.729
They're so forgiving and nice.

00:01:55.729 --> 00:01:58.899
It'll be fine," and she was also like, "Now,
no offense, but it's not like that many people

00:01:58.899 --> 00:02:03.189
outside of Nerdfighteria are going to see
your video because, you know, it's about agricultural

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:04.570
yields in the developing world.

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:07.730
It's not- it's not going to catch fire on
Tumblr," and then, of course, within a day,

00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:11.700
Bill Gates, the head of The World Food Program,
and the foreign minister of Ethiopia had all

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:12.700
seen the video.

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:15.670
Now, they were all very nice about it, but
you know that secretly, they were like, "Hectacre?"

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:18.970
So, it's one thing to look like a grapefruit
in front of Nerdfighteria, Hank; they've come

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:21.290
to expect it, but Bill Gates?

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:22.290
Mortifying.

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:25.100
Now, when mortification is something that
you're doing entirely to yourself, as it was

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:28.750
in this instance, there can be something useful
about it maybe, like I want to seem like the

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:31.910
kind of person who knows a lot about agriculture
or the kind of person who can get through

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:36.100
a two-person book signing without alienating
the audience, and mortification can be your

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:39.960
ideas about yourself being challenged by the
reality of yourself, and that's not such a

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:43.660
bad thing, but when other people try to mortify
you, when they say cruel things about you

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:47.650
to make you feel embarrassed of yourself or
when they read your diary or share pictures

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:50.010
that you wish to remain private, that's very
different.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:52.620
I don't think anything is gained there, and
a lot is lost.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:56.230
Hank, I don't think any of us can navigate
the world always having to be our public selves

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:59.180
even when in private because I think we'd
go mad.

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:02.200
Now, God knows I'll never say "hectacre" in
a Vlogbrothers video again, but if I can't

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:06.460
say it at home, I'm seriously kind of doomed
because then I'd lose touch with my real and

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:11.340
actual self, which, for all of us, is necessarily
different from the selves we project in public.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:15.010
We all act differently in private than we
do in school or at our jobs or on Tumblr.

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:18.710
Now, Hank, I can't speak for everyone, but
I for one wouldn't want to have to be on Tumblr

00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:19.710
all the time.

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:22.440
Well- no, no, no, because my kids don't have
Tumblrs.

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:23.440
Yet.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:26.900
Anyway, Hank, if you've learned one thing
from being my brother, I hope it is this:

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:27.900
hectare!

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:29.200
Rhymes with nectar almost.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:30.740
Rhymes with lecture.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:31.740
Not really.

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:35.670
Those, by the way, are called slant rhymes,
he said confidently, feeling a whisper of

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:36.670
doubt.

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:36.675
I'll see you on Friday.

